Safety section	UNKNOWN STEP	## Safety / regression evidence
Safety section	UNKNOWN STEP	
Safety section	UNKNOWN STEP	- **Contracts and risk:** High-risk ownership/concurrency change affecting `durable-ack`, `recovery-generation`, `reader-snapshot`, `historical-input` and `query-meaning`. Preserve admission, formats, isolation and configured durability; intentionally add explicit handle outcomes and documented disposal/error contracts. [API and migration guide](https://github.com/JKamsker/LiteDB/blob/12a057424/docs/transaction-handles.md).
Safety section	UNKNOWN STEP	- **Prior states and interactions:** Direct/Shared; ordinary, legacy and handle calls; concurrent handles and live cursors; pooled hosts; maintenance/close; encrypted/historical files; external writers; abandoned sessions; reused wrappers with fresh cores. Inherited recovery, compatibility and fuzz coverage remains required.
Safety section	UNKNOWN STEP	- **Failure outcomes:** Acknowledged commits survive the stated fault models; rolled-back writes stay absent; uncertain commits remain indeterminate. Close cancels blocked admission before draining work, and maintenance permits existing dependencies to finish. Cleanup preserves its own errors without repeating a peer's fatal error. External writes become visible after reacquisition and cold reopen. Shared parent final checkpointing remains explicitly best-effort; pin/child errors propagate after cleanup.
Safety section	UNKNOWN STEP	- **Evidence:** Eighteen registered comparisons: eight against actual `560529066`, three against actual `eb01f346e`, three against actual `e821ae747`, two against actual `f95f0b0cd`, and two against published packages. Permanent guards, registered fault hooks, coverage dispositions and runtime/platform checks accompany them. The current production tree passes **675 relevant tests on each net8/net10**, zero failures/skips; independent combined reader-cleanup review passes **54 cases per runtime**. The exact final pin harness passes six cases per runtime; the broader native/process harness passes 30 per runtime, with its source-state limits retained. Counts overlap. All production targets build; net462/net481 tests compile. All **101 CI-policy tests** pass. [Full CI](https://github.com/JKamsker/LiteDB/actions/runs/36720069909), [18 proofs](https://github.com/JKamsker/LiteDB/actions/runs/36720069368), [Fuzz](https://github.com/JKamsker/LiteDB/actions/runs/36720069340), [index compatibility](https://github.com/JKamsker/LiteDB/actions/runs/36720069466) and [Shared production comparisons](https://github.com/JKamsker/LiteDB/actions/runs/36720069436) must finish on this exact head, including actual CLR 4 and native platforms. [Finite gate and detailed dispositions](https://github.com/JKamsker/LiteDB/blob/12a057424/docs/transaction-handles-safety-review.md); final immutable evidence publication remains pending.
Safety section	UNKNOWN STEP	- **Coverage delta and residual concerns:** Replacements and CI/harness changes are recorded in the ledger; no new quarantine. Native partitions preserve the exact selection and reject missing/duplicate/empty results; discovery now matches VSTest case sensitivity. The pin-progress test deliberately replaces a combined startup/throughput budget with bounded startup and completed-write progress, a 60-second overall cap, held-pin negative controls and exact cold-state assertions. Its original Windows timeout remains **unknown in origin**, with no retrospective deadlock or runner-slowdown claim. The strongest counterexample is close/maintenance preventing a continuation it needs; forced interleavings, genuine-error controls and before/after proofs address it. Existing quarantines remain gaps. Evidence covers exceptions, process death and modeled persistence faults, not physical power removal. This is the affected PR's finite #3034 gate, not completion of the whole roadmap or repository protection/merge-queue configuration; post-merge validation is configured, not claimed executed.
Safety section	UNKNOWN STEP	
Safety section	UNKNOWN STEP	## Behavior and review dispositions
Safety section	UNKNOWN STEP	
Safety section	UNKNOWN STEP	`BeginTransaction()` creates an independent handle. Its collections, queries and readers stay bound across sequential thread handoff; ordinary calls remain independent. Overlapping use and callback reentry reject before execution, and completed/abandoned handles release session/application ownership.
Safety section	UNKNOWN STEP	
Safety section	UNKNOWN STEP	The only integrated performance option is **Shared holder-worker and child `SharedEngine` wrapper reuse**. **Every transaction still releases native writer ownership and closes/reopens its storage core.** No coherent page cache survives an external writer. Plain/encrypted tests establish actual reuse, distinct core identity, external writer progress, refreshed indexed reads and cold reopen. Idle resources, coordination participation and mode-admission leases retire correctly; password/collation changes invalidate the wrapper. No group commit, batching, IPC service, retained native lock/core, atomic `SessionLifetime`, single-bound-scope or inline-close experiment is included.
Safety section	UNKNOWN STEP	
Safety section	UNKNOWN STEP	CI corrections retain their failed evidence: three local-feed repros now route through the dedicated proof workflow; an existing callback test now expects the documented immediate close refusal while still checking the injected I/O error and native exclusion. The previous Linux ARM64/net10 allocation assertion observed 6,056 bytes; **its cause remains unknown**. The test now isolates measurement on a dedicated thread, retains the exact zero-byte requirement and 1,000 measured publications, verifies state publication, and includes an escaping-allocation control that fails the zero assertion at 24,000 bytes. The 19065 ARM64/net10 job passed both strict-zero and positive-control tests with runtime/architecture guards; the successor still requires its own full matrix. No measured retry/discard or threshold relaxation is used. The later Windows callback-test cleanup failure retains its exact log/TRX: the 100 ms probe was accidentally applied to normal teardown. The corrected test keeps that limit only for self-dependent callback cases and restores the prior deadline in `finally`; safe controls use the normal deadline. It passes 18 cases per runtime and still detects all six original failures on real `e821` per runtime, with six safe controls passing. Independent review found no weakened oracle.
Safety section	UNKNOWN STEP	
Safety section	UNKNOWN STEP	The original eight enumerated production findings have fixes and permanent guards: Shared-close admission cancellation; maintenance fairness; raw-close waiter cancellation; nested Shared-owner begin rejection; disposed-child validation before abort handling; disposal racing session close; peer-fatal versus genuine cleanup errors; and stale-sort cleanup under writable ownership. Subsequent review found and fixed deferred-fatal, child capture/admission and scope-handoff variants. The audit retains original regression failures and mutation controls, including failed intermediate fixes and documented harness corrections. Source-specific provenance and the unavailable first facade setup-failure report are explicitly identified; the corrected proof reports and binaries are retained.
Safety section	UNKNOWN STEP	
Safety section	UNKNOWN STEP	The [follow-up review](https://github.com/JKamsker/LiteDB/pull/133#pullrequestreview-5364329616) and [additional findings](https://github.com/JKamsker/LiteDB/pull/133#issuecomment-5908951510) have these dispositions:
Safety section	UNKNOWN STEP	
Safety section	UNKNOWN STEP	| Finding | Resolution and evidence |
Safety section	UNKNOWN STEP	| --- | --- |
Safety section	UNKNOWN STEP	| Pin-close error skips Shared cleanup | Finish independent retirement, preserve primary/secondary errors, and preserve surviving-reader leases/snapshots. A real Linux OS I/O fault reproduces on `eb01` and passes the correction; a peer writer progresses before the surviving reader ends. |
Safety section	UNKNOWN STEP	| Shared parent final-checkpoint contract | Narrow documentation to its historical best-effort behavior; parent-only fault tests retain WAL and recover committed indexed data. Pin/child errors still propagate. |
Safety section	UNKNOWN STEP	| Raw-close callback dependency; ordinary callback self-wait | Reject fresh work behind close and same-handle callback lock self-waits; preserve unrelated operations and idle-handle handoff. Intermediate scope-release and refused-close/native-release defects have explicit guards; restore context before admission handoff and refuse raw callback disposal before mutating ownership. Raw disposal still cannot bound an arbitrary application callback that never returns. |
Safety section	UNKNOWN STEP	| Cancellable mutex polling | Windows uses blocking `WaitAny` with a scoped cancellation event/registration; Unix retains bounded waits because named-mutex `WaitAny` is unsupported there. Uncontended admission allocates neither. Cancellation, release and owner-death controls pass; source inspection alone establishes no performance attribution. |
Safety section	UNKNOWN STEP	| Cached `ReadTransform` | Bounded allegation refuted by original/current public probes and four new guards: constructor settings are snapshotted for ordinary calls and handles; the retained delegate's mutable target remains visible. |
Safety section	UNKNOWN STEP	| Hypothetical pre-persist Commit leak | Unconfirmed in supported public paths; four precondition/outcome/retry controls pass without changing Commit semantics. |
Safety section	UNKNOWN STEP	| Diagnostics and structural nits | Zero `ThreadID` is diagnostic. The unpruned per-path managed registry predates reuse and retains no native ownership/application graph. Scheduler duplication, unsupported drop/rename API shape and existing encoding are outside this correction. |
Safety section	UNKNOWN STEP	
Safety section	UNKNOWN STEP	The newest review is addressed by checking all executing mutex-dependent cores before close changes state, preserving native protection when teardown is refused, and draining operations outside the Shared lock needed by callbacks. A closing-core fence prevents fresh opens from using a draining core. Forced owner-exit, pin, unleased-snapshot and fresh-open interleavings include plain/encrypted native exclusion, external writer progress and cold indexed/sentinel checks. Facade disposal from an owned ordinary-reader callback now refuses immediately; independent leased readers and non-owning facades retain their valid behavior. The guide also identifies the last ordinary reader's `Dispose()` as an idle pin-close error path. Actual before/fixed proofs retain the original failures, intermediate race and corrected plain/encrypted harness conditions.
Safety section	UNKNOWN STEP	
Safety section	UNKNOWN STEP	The [ordinary-reader follow-up](https://github.com/JKamsker/LiteDB/pull/133#pullrequestreview-5366098967) adds two corrections. Last-reader cleanup still requests native retirement but avoids joining a core that is draining the current callback, including owner-exit optional checkpointing; committed WAL remains authoritative and the concurrent disposer still receives actual close errors. Leased-reader self-disposal now refuses before mutating the cursor or disposal latch, allowing a valid retry after callback unwind. Tests retain readers strongly, prove admission release and indexed cold recovery, and cover both snapshot construction paths in plain/encrypted databases. No native lifetime is shortened and no scheduler is added.
Safety section	UNKNOWN STEP	
Safety section	UNKNOWN STEP	The separate follow-up pin-loop fixture now observes startup before pin establishment, native admission, and all twenty separately committed records within its unchanged ten-second post-go budget. A live-owner check prevents owner death from mimicking yielding; held-pin controls require no committed progress. Typed retention manifests track all child PIDs and refuse copies until host and children exit, keeping files on their original temporary volume. Native crash capture also retains all 24 deliberately failed fixture sets and 20 recovery markers in controls. The original ARM failure occurred in the initial raw-file snapshot after awaited child death, before recovery reopen; its lock owner cannot be reconstructed from the missing fixture. A later green run cannot explain either original failure.
Safety section	UNKNOWN STEP	
Safety section	UNKNOWN STEP	Public compatibility: typed/BSON collections, Include/vector queries, indexes and `$cols`/`$indexes` metadata are bound; read-only handles cannot write. SQL, FileStorage and maintenance are not handle APIs; drop/rename and external/system query I/O reject. Unsupported custom/coordinated/decorated providers and Shared private storage/caller streams reject handles; Direct caller streams remain supported. Existing interface implementers gain no required members. Legacy signatures remain with CS0618 warnings and a documented warnings-as-errors migration. Known committed outcomes survive cleanup errors; disposed facade access throws `ObjectDisposedException`. Parameterless Shared begin remains unbounded; opt-in timeout/cancellation bounds begin admission separately from collection `TIMEOUT`.
Safety section	UNKNOWN STEP	
